=== ROLLBACK ===
PER_PROMPT:
  Each remediation prompt is atomic: one source file (e.g., internal/api/charging_handler.go) plus its sibling _test.go. To revert any single fix:
    git revert <commit-sha-of-that-prompt>
  This restores both the source file and the test file to their pre-prompt state without touching any other prompt's work.

PREDECESSOR_CHAIN_PROTECTION:
  Every remediation prompt includes a `predecessor` check in its preflight (verifies the previous prompt's log shows STATUS=DONE). Reverting a middle prompt does NOT silently break later prompts -- the next time a later prompt is re-run, its predecessor-DONE check will fail and the prompt will refuse to execute, alerting the operator that the chain is broken.

PHASE_LEVEL_ROLLBACK:
  Recommended pre-execution step (operator-driven, NOT enforced by this prompt):
    git tag phase-41-pre <HEAD before prompt 0010>
    git push origin phase-41-pre
  To roll back the entire phase:
    git reset --hard phase-41-pre
    git push --force-with-lease origin refactor/signals-rewrite
  This reverts all 297 remediation commits in one shot. Operators should ONLY do this on a non-shared branch.

LOG_RETENTION:
  All 299 prompt logs are committed under .github/prompts/db-refactor/logs/. They are git-add -f because .gitignore:89 excludes /.github/prompts. Logs persist across rollbacks because they are part of the survey commit, not the remediation commits. A phase-41-pre reset removes them; operators who want forensic history should `git format-patch phase-41-pre..HEAD -- .github/prompts/db-refactor/logs/phase-41-*` before resetting.

DATABASE_ROLLBACK_NOT_APPLICABLE:
  Phase-41 makes NO migrations and NO schema changes. There is no DB rollback story because there is no DB forward step. Any source-level changes that talk to the DB use the same migration version as before.

